#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221144 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "03fbfe2c-b12f-5220-9fae-e0dfe210a505" :question/math-academy-id "e-21857" :question/problem "Find the Fourier series of a particular solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'-2y=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim 3 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{12}{n^2\\pi}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a particular solution has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) + b_n \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y'_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-2(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -{a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{0}=3 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n}=\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation,\n\n$$\na_0=-3,\n$$\n\nwhich means the constant term is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation,\n$b_n=-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}.$\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}\\right)\\dfrac{n\\pi}{3}-2a_n=\\dfrac{12}{n^2\\pi}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\na_n=-\\dfrac{216}{n^2\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+36)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $b_n$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{216}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼-\\frac{3}{2} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{216}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221145 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "3944f6e4-a3a8-5bad-87c5-18227ce223bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21858" :question/problem "Find the Fourier series of a particular solution of the differential equation $y''-y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim 1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: We may assume that all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Because the forcing function has only a constant term and sine terms, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y''_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty -b_n n^2\\pi^2 \\sin(n\\pi x)\n-\n\\left( \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x) \\right)\n=\n1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(-n^2\\pi^2-1)\\sin(n\\pi x)\n=\n1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/assets/249067c54bdeee50fdddb787db0d9fc6e95eeddd7cbf322cb425c08a2a25d97c.png)\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \n-1\n+ \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\:\n\\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^3\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+1)}\n\\cdot\n\\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221147 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/id #uuid "926606c2-1e1a-5aab-8f2e-826163a204a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21875" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $y'' + 9\\pi^2 y = f(x),$ where the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nWhat harmonic of the forcing function does the natural frequency of the system match?\nAnd what is the resonant component of a particular solution?" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+9\\pi^2 y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of\n\n$$\n\\omega=\\sqrt{9\\pi^2}=3\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $2$-periodic function $\\left(L=1\\right)$ are\n\n$$\n\\omega_n=\\dfrac{n\\pi}{1}=n\\pi.\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have $\\omega_3=3\\pi.$ Since $\\omega_3=\\omega,$ the natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function. This is the answer to the first question.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\nf_3(x)=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_{p_3}=x(A\\cos(3\\pi x)+B\\sin(3\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))+x(-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y''_{p_3}$ and $y_{p_3}$ into the equation $y''+9\\pi^2 y=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\left[-6\\pi A\\sin(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\cos(3\\pi x)-9\\pi^2 y_{p_3}\\right]+9\\pi^2 y_{p_3}=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-6\\pi A\\sin(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\cos(3\\pi x)=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074986/assets/599de8dda7f476da630651310a373eae36280c75676a44da91105c9a1751456b.png)\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_{p_3}=-\\dfrac{x}{54\\pi}\\cos(3\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa674a96-5fad-5391-9ecd-7207de293f9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5(− 1)^{n})/(n^{2}π(nπ + 5))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220788} {:answer/id #uuid "f9acec18-0b78-5a76-b640-05ca4f225236" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (25(− 1)^{n})/(nπ^{2}(nπ + 5))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220789} {:answer/id #uuid "74f30165-bd51-51ea-a133-85c243830ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5(− 1)^{n})/(n^{2}π(n^{2}π^{2} + 25))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74f30165-bd51-51ea-a133-85c243830ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5(− 1)^{n})/(n^{2}π(n^{2}π^{2} + 25))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220790} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d9a57ed-8530-5c19-90db-08bc23e6955f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220787} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2ca3dc9-a32d-5f02-981c-e89cae88e801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (5(− 1)^{n})/(n^{2}π^{2}(n^{2}π^{2} + 25))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220792} {:answer/id #uuid "c84cf2bc-3717-5ce7-992c-c5cf23e100ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((− 1)^{n})/(n^{2}(n^{2}π^{2} + 25))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220793} {:answer/id #uuid "e597a9bb-eae7-5a94-b367-cad1ff2d843b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− ((− 1)^{n})/(n(n^{2}π^{2} + 25))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e597a9bb-eae7-5a94-b367-cad1ff2d843b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− ((− 1)^{n})/(n(n^{2}π^{2} + 25))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220794} :answer-field/id #uuid "c518bf4d-4948-5e28-9485-810284e8c4f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7431cef2-4a56-5a41-81e1-4185e67efb64" :question/math-academy-id "q-342203" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}+5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))+{b}_{n}\\cdot n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)) \\\\  & \\,+5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)) \\\\  & \\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({b}_{n}n\\pi +5{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-{a}_{n}n\\pi +5{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}=0 \\\\ {b}_{n}n\\pi +5{a}_{n}=0 \\\\ -{a}_{n}n\\pi +5{b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the second equation, \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the third equation gives\n\n$$\n-(-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{5})n\\pi +5{b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{5}\\cdot \\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83c2fe7c-768a-5095-b7ae-118309ccc444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4)/(nπ(n^{2}π^{2} + 4))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220796} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1c6c3e-8b0d-54ed-a547-d12c23276d88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (16)/(n^{2}π(n^{2}π^{2} + 4))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220797} {:answer/id #uuid "44571791-bb0d-5624-9acd-4a57a2022b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8)/(n^{2}π(n^{2}π^{2} + 4))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f1c6c3e-8b0d-54ed-a547-d12c23276d88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (16)/(n^{2}π(n^{2}π^{2} + 4))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220797} :answer-field/id #uuid "17645703-ca70-5ca4-a08d-b18a066f0879" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220795} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7c55d77-2cbd-52af-8560-fff44a4ff93d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220800} {:answer/id #uuid "c854de7f-2540-5336-ba63-ed1d892af5de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220801} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb14614-cf3e-5b89-82a2-00bcc90f6d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220802} {:answer/id #uuid "71ac7490-f533-55b6-bbe9-767a45ecd547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71ac7490-f533-55b6-bbe9-767a45ecd547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220803} :answer-field/id #uuid "487be717-7b8a-50cf-980b-9d7e78fbc4ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220799} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e848dfbd-0bc1-5c4f-a87e-16697b22a8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8)/(n(n^{2}π^{2} + 4))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220805} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff377ba-3eea-5115-bf4c-0aff41450faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4)/(n^{2}(n^{2}π^{2} + 4))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220806} {:answer/id #uuid "557f067f-1fae-5cbb-9ce0-79e70f642280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(16)/(n^{2}(n^{2}π^{2} + 4))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e848dfbd-0bc1-5c4f-a87e-16697b22a8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8)/(n(n^{2}π^{2} + 4))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220805} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbba3f79-e33b-5892-ad18-cde9a668efe2" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0f6d5f3-5213-5096-b128-e5b4d23a6bdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-342229" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})) \\\\  & \\,-(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}-{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}-{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-1 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}-{a}_{n}=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}-{b}_{n}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation, ${b}_{n}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2})\\frac{n\\pi }{2}-{a}_{n}=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{8}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff3fbff4-b497-5614-9eb6-7b9a63f1829b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9(− 1)^{n})/(n^{2}π(144 − n^{2}π^{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220831} {:answer/id #uuid "081d2d10-2c6f-5f24-8c4c-f1f8780020e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3(− 1)^{n})/(nπ^{2}(4 + nπ))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220832} {:answer/id #uuid "3aabedc3-6770-58b6-af7f-40e9432a6d47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (3(− 1)^{n})/(16 + n^{2}π^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220833} {:answer/id #uuid "6feaa1e2-98d2-5960-9b1f-94704af30a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (9(− 1)^{n})/(n^{2}π^{2}(16 − n^{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff3fbff4-b497-5614-9eb6-7b9a63f1829b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9(− 1)^{n})/(n^{2}π(144 − n^{2}π^{2}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220831} :answer-field/id #uuid "8410ec55-d2ac-5404-90ba-cb8ca780851c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2c6150b-3687-5b67-8114-c6babfa1ec19" :question/math-academy-id "q-343373" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}+16y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+16({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms to find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(16-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\frac{144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{n}(144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}{9}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ca4617b-f6ee-568f-b057-242d7ac5e556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381560} {:answer/id #uuid "3833274d-fa33-50ed-8932-cdad99d6d0c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381561} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6f1919-d39c-5590-b5a5-602330e004b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381562} {:answer/id #uuid "641b8192-2a7b-50d0-bc99-0aed731710db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3833274d-fa33-50ed-8932-cdad99d6d0c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381561} :answer-field/id #uuid "78b69f20-28f7-5099-8662-a1577cb30e86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381559} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e33415-427a-5cc4-ac71-56549adcae0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381565} {:answer/id #uuid "7a34cae3-944e-5243-aa7a-44c3d12a314f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381566} {:answer/id #uuid "f3588e71-76f2-568a-98e8-88c47183734f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{24}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a34cae3-944e-5243-aa7a-44c3d12a314f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381566} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d3297ce-78d0-534e-8eef-1168af69890b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381564} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e989a55-438f-53ea-b680-ef15964cdd62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381569} {:answer/id #uuid "6319c66d-3810-52a9-88f9-716222545ff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381570} {:answer/id #uuid "89f3fc3c-8393-5d8e-b85b-dd1bd0f8692c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e989a55-438f-53ea-b680-ef15964cdd62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381569} :answer-field/id #uuid "251e6c1e-ef05-56ab-875d-aaace058c92f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f846e2b8-ed1e-5269-a8b4-5680f6871bb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343854" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-2y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-2(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -2\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n}=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation, ${b}_{n}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6})\\frac{n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n}=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{12}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5ef46f2-6c2c-5d49-bafe-ce8216a7246f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381574} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb78af8-3484-5d19-8032-3b65c5f8d3f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381575} {:answer/id #uuid "292592f7-cd45-5bfd-803d-f135da130be8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381576} {:answer/id #uuid "dbdc1ed8-b4f7-5084-b65d-37d239aec12e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5ef46f2-6c2c-5d49-bafe-ce8216a7246f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381574} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0fce4f7-108e-5517-99b1-55931df0799d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381573} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d161ed7-b4f7-5fbc-9a69-12c4ec604f88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381579} {:answer/id #uuid "570f13d8-c029-55d0-9230-12b3590b0f03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381580} {:answer/id #uuid "e57e4008-8b19-5b09-bd63-f103f1f4d62b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e57e4008-8b19-5b09-bd63-f103f1f4d62b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381581} :answer-field/id #uuid "13d02dbf-ed3a-5530-b306-bc6f7c104cea" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381578} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6efb9e5f-ad09-51c7-b7f2-bfa01cca37ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{54}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381583} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc5cc3e-ad84-532f-83c4-912c2959a211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381584} {:answer/id #uuid "a71cd3d0-5491-5993-8812-53d33c1a2a38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a71cd3d0-5491-5993-8812-53d33c1a2a38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381585} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ec2f0c3-c2f8-5497-803d-b2b5552d41ff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15f5a0b9-1297-5188-a047-103209518fed" :question/math-academy-id "q-343855" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-3y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-3(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{b}_{n}=\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=\\frac{4}{3},$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, from the second equation, ${b}_{n}=\\frac{9{a}_{n}}{n\\pi }.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3(\\frac{9{a}_{n}}{n\\pi })=\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{9{a}_{n}}{n\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{n\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼\\frac{2}{3} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:a