uuid "34233318-1ac8-5e24-a0de-a79b21805ef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381597} {:answer/id #uuid "0e09acf2-8e75-52cd-9451-b528d13138d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381598} {:answer/id #uuid "1f59247a-34a5-5822-8d73-376cfd49374a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e09acf2-8e75-52cd-9451-b528d13138d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381598} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cd16b97-7928-5399-827a-be2ba919fb9b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2c46dfd-7116-546d-b775-da0e3f310626" :question/math-academy-id "q-343860" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -5\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-5 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{a}_{n}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{b}_{n}=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the second equation, ${b}_{n}=\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi }.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5(\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi })=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225} \\\\  & =\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "066adb77-4fef-5896-b3f3-086c22f88d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381602} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3622df-d4a6-5864-973b-8ec41a593bc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381603} {:answer/id #uuid "98660098-67af-5109-ab0e-710d46e600d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381604} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b0bf88-c54c-5056-8eaa-68b895675469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98660098-67af-5109-ab0e-710d46e600d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381604} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6b83213-63a7-503b-9bd4-c8cea8009c7f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec7e24e7-62d8-5d3d-951b-9cb4a6f517ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-343875" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}+9y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+9({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms to find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(9-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\frac{36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{n}(36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}{4}=\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe47edba-00aa-584b-8ca3-a251a94b0352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381608} {:answer/id #uuid "04db3ded-57d5-550b-b2e9-0eee5e89897f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381609} {:answer/id #uuid "76aa0dbc-a2f2-5ff8-98b5-492d97d93eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381610} {:answer/id #uuid "32c06bb3-9d1d-54ec-ad5e-0ca4c5c7515a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76aa0dbc-a2f2-5ff8-98b5-492d97d93eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381610} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3c682fc-f8d2-5ed9-8618-f9eaa6e8b577" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381607} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fd3a2ad-a419-532f-8b34-564c4168e912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381613} {:answer/id #uuid "16a79094-393e-58cd-b974-85f07a856063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381614} {:answer/id #uuid "37d0c84a-9d94-596f-bd1c-b1447c0be2b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381615} {:answer/id #uuid "e2aceaa9-ddc3-5b43-a3e6-46151ebdeb00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2aceaa9-ddc3-5b43-a3e6-46151ebdeb00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381616} :answer-field/id #uuid "0466d797-bb22-5dec-803e-f22ffb2205f6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55973a14-e29e-556f-a95e-57060b444dca" :question/math-academy-id "q-343879" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-4y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})-4(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))=4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-4\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})=4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=4 \\\\ {a}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-4)=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-1 \\\\ {a}_{n}=\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+16)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+16)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a791155c-1e31-508a-a5d2-90770343d5a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381619} {:answer/id #uuid "15e9a0a1-55c4-5466-8c46-243fa4338f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381620} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8485d1-445b-59c0-a899-20ff8429dfa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381621} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1c52e7-97a1-5810-80bf-08777ab3b3f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e8485d1-445b-59c0-a899-20ff8429dfa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381621} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1d2f1eb-29c7-54bd-ab94-2bc0a6fe1e3d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381618} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c367e352-84c2-56c6-8595-3bbcc3cf5990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381624} {:answer/id #uuid "9b0356be-f83c-52fb-843e-488beb54413f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381625} {:answer/id #uuid "86e8e0ca-f8e0-5938-a0fa-c35075bf225d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381626} {:answer/id #uuid "8657d2c4-110e-5171-bc38-de39b36df3bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b0356be-f83c-52fb-843e-488beb54413f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381625} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f6977f8-a4b0-505f-aa0e-68056761b03f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2516cd4-03d4-5396-a39e-539760feeafc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343880" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-3y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})-3(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}-3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=3 \\\\ {a}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}-3)=\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-1 \\\\ {a}_{n}=\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5bfec6a-f17a-5db4-a473-c51224c0f8db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381630} {:answer/id #uuid "4a14f0f8-6046-57d9-bd6f-0bc4f4f8ff0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381631} {:answer/id #uuid "5921e40b-d10c-5e57-a965-29a82169eda1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381632} {:answer/id #uuid "5b90f063-ac9b-5d48-8a50-a2e8476a7669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5921e40b-d10c-5e57-a965-29a82169eda1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381632} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8455c88-8e27-5930-b34b-270604bb7cd3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381629} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f40dabd9-1149-55aa-924b-e62a4f62ef84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381635} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0227ce-7472-5552-a503-d2df5f18edfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381636} {:answer/id #uuid "eaed5426-09ec-5b59-b138-e929b39c6649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381637} {:answer/id #uuid "d6903c66-84b6-5cd4-9f49-eccc11873678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc0227ce-7472-5552-a503-d2df5f18edfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381636} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f3bc728-0d94-590c-8dc4-d41f07c89a5c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d229eaa3-4713-5a48-9c69-bb77cc634b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-343883" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-8y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)-8(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))=8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-8\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-8)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)=8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-8\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=8 \\\\ {b}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-8)=\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-1 \\\\ {b}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba2ec886-54bf-5294-b6a0-d9fe9bad7746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381641} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3bd359-1afa-5ae7-b1be-45252c3cecdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381642} {:answer/id #uuid "3eda0f41-de30-5369-af27-7cb1c9dbbd91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381643} {:answer/id #uuid "7cea4a75-9401-5fb9-b34d-b9c14f746e61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cea4a75-9401-5fb9-b34d-b9c14f746e61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381644} :answer-field/id #uuid "76115cd8-b45d-59ac-95e3-85798f55de7c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381640} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79b2cd31-8866-53ce-9b1c-dc313ca74db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381646} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5e3bc1-66a5-5c6d-9f61-a0b65e0a2d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381647} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ebf9a8-da6f-5810-af12-926da63ec7c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381648} {:answer/id #uuid "894a6a5d-25a5-59b6-9098-42f54e546b25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{216\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2ebf9a8-da6f-5810-af12-926da63ec7c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381648} :answer-field/id #uuid "2df8088e-3b88-58aa-9b7c-ecb28ffcdc78" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d694cb78-fd17-5d72-bd5e-fb91875a20a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343891" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $1$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{36{\\pi }^{2}}=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $1$-periodic function $(L=\\frac{1}{2})$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=6\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))+x(-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-12\\pi A=0 \\\\ 12\\pi B=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{108\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6b63276-04f3-5a23-b919-82ffbb94fcfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381652} {:answer/id #uuid "f949596b-312b-58f8-b914-3b0e4c62063f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381653} {:answer/id #uuid "06a98246-3fb2-5e95-ac75-a127da6306be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381654} {:answer/id #uuid "c300fb27-9a8d-5e54-b24f-e875335924d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06a98246-3fb2-5e95-ac75-a127da6306be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381654} :answer-field/id #uuid "f38fa537-1c55-5839-ad1f-351198d778f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381651} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a61ac39c-6643-56e5-83d2-bb227d247124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381657} {:answer/id #uuid "967a0ecb-67b8-5341-bdba-ee19b52d0132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381658} {:answer/id #uuid "a4161067-82e1-59c3-8c6b-bab1c1861f51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381659} {:answer/id #uuid "e7029898-32ed-5470-948f-c78e59fb6b81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7029898-32ed-5470-948f-c78e59fb6b81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381660} :answer-field/id #uuid "68872846-8771-5f5c-a8ed-ce5688b7f88a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd94ef93-5daf-566f-92be-7aef938749ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-343899" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}=\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))+x(-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi A=0 \\\\ 2\\pi B=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{8\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3b4b37f-3f6d-5374-a408-afc4606c7ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381663} {:answer/id #uuid "27bccb4b-240e-55ac-b874-069f18fd814d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381664} {:answer/id #uuid "20a6bc97-0304-5d0d-bba2-342e94256fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381665} {:answer/id #uuid "1aa80f3d-478c-5e6c-9197-7f8c3a6785d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27bccb4b-240e-55ac-b874-069f18fd814d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381664} :answer-field/id #uuid "abe806f4-60a3-5c2f-a80d-ba6977c66f38" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381662} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c3516fc-dd7e-5c23-a248-7ce79410773f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381668} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fa1be6-199e-5483-b653-995ed92936bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381669} {:answer/id #uuid "ba718f78-8344-5633-a867-a9dd9ad6f59a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381670} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7cbffa-a1d3-59e5-8917-0b87c231c396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba718f78-8344-5633-a867-a9dd9ad6f59a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381670} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6186985-abb4-5505-893c-a4117c25a351" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69dfefcc-d6fa-5562-9f09-af1265e69934" :question/math-academy-id "q-343901" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\pi A=0 \\\\ \\pi B=6\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{6}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f732854-1558-5887-9e86-919e4bd92ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381674} {:answer/id #uuid "8d639b10-d3bd-56db-b9ac-f3349e568fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381675} {:answer/id #uuid "f8bba97f-4fc3-5cf7-aab9-ead5fc18f755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381676} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2e9bf6-8db7-5d65-ace9-6cbaf2160c4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8bba97f-4fc3-5cf7-aab9-ead5fc18f755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381676} :answer-field/id #uuid "456e78da-1c0b-52b0-8fa2-d71b94af576f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381673} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bcb50a0-60c8-5f0c-a6cb-ff58c5dfd912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15x}{32\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381679} {:answer/id #uuid "822ea600-bc89-5ae8-bb80-462565ab5b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381680} {:answer/id #uuid "0bad6c6e-8edc-51e7-963a-ec22db5b38a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381681} {:answer/id #uuid "2f0d7b72-3541-58f2-a53b-ca6dae745357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f0d7b72-3541-58f2-a53b-ca6dae745357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381682} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a2e750b-aed1-5faa-931d-ec930b8f4ac6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6fe7a72d-6273-5f21-8d68-5569740a0dae" :question/math-academy-id "q-343905" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x),$ where the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼-3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))+x(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{3}B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}A=-\\frac{15}{16\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f567e0-232a-5790-9d10-9f6c15131d3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381685} {:answer/id #uuid "b08a12f8-4404-5312-8652-c2dafe28e96f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381686} {:answer/id #uuid "e7919b65-bb4d-5e3b-a8c4-71d8b4de9f88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381687} {:answer/id #uuid "8d578bd3-2c90-5731-a3c2-84a656947504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7919b65-bb4d-5e3b-a8c4-71d8b4de9f88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381687} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bd429fc-c483-522f-b5e3-49c453753a47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381684} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84f74110-9fd6-5af5-b6ae-3d050319823c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381690} {:answer/id #uuid "b3456568-b801-5727-8c60-a83c4e4c0313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381691} {:answer/id #uuid "f96d3d28-4e76-50cf-bce8-2952223a1754" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381692} {:answer/id #uuid "7c92a2d2-2015-5f3c-9f0e-ad698943fef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f96d3d28-4e76-50cf-bce8-2952223a1754" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381692} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b6c874c-5ecb-53fb-91cb-75ba7c271a69" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca918037-2aef-5d68-8136-9e95ce20b852" :question/math-academy-id "q-343907" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}=\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))+x(-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi A=1 \\\\ 2\\pi B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}A=-\\frac{1}{2\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$"}]}
